---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import { pages } from '../../lib/seo';

const seo = pages.altPoe;
---
<ContentLayout
  seo={seo}
  kicker="Comparison"
  heading="Poe alternative — multi-model chat for Android"
  lead="Open-source, BYOK, and local history versus Poe's subscription model hub."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Alternatives', path: '/alternatives' }]}
  related={[
    { label: 'Models', href: '/models' },
    { label: 'OpenRouter', href: '/openrouter' },
    { label: 'All alternatives', href: '/alternatives' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Poe aggregates many bots behind one account. EchoFlow is an <strong>open-source Poe alternative</strong>
    and <strong>multi-model alternative to Poe</strong> for users who prefer Android-native UX,
    API-key pricing, and conversations stored locally.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable
    competitorName="Poe"
    rows={[
      { label: 'Model access', echoflow: 'OpenRouter/BYOK—you choose providers', competitor: 'Poe subscription unlocks bot access' },
      { label: 'Platform', echoflow: 'Android-first open-source app', competitor: 'Web + mobile apps (Quora)' },
      { label: 'Cost model', echoflow: 'Pay providers directly', competitor: 'Poe subscription tiers' },
      { label: 'Open source', echoflow: 'Yes', competitor: 'No' },
      { label: 'Chat storage', echoflow: 'Local on device', competitor: 'Poe account cloud' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Poe can be convenient if you want a curated bot marketplace with one bill. EchoFlow suits
    power users who want <strong>chat with different AI models</strong> under their own keys and data control.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
